<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kim Il Sung</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1912">1912</a>–<a href="/years/1994">1994</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kim Il Sung was a Korean communist who fought as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> and later served in the Soviet army. Brought back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> by the Soviet occupation in 1945, he became chairman of the Provisional People's Committee for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a> in 1946 and leader of the Democratic People's Republic of Korea from 1948. He launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a> in 1950 and ruled until his death, founding a dynasty continued by his son and grandson.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kim-il-sung.jpg" alt="Kim Il Sung" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kim_Il-sung_in_1950_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Succession is uncertain; collective leadership has repeatedly given way to personal rule and the cults of leaders such as Stalin, Mao and <b>Kim Il Sung</b>; and officials who owe their posts to their superiors have every reason to hide failures.</blockquote><a href="/government/one-party-state">One-party state · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Kim Il Sung</b> took up the image to urge the workers of North Korea to bring the country out of the rubble of the Korean War and build a more prosperous one in a short time.</blockquote><a href="/songs/chollima-on-the-wing">Chollima on the Wing</a></li><li><blockquote>It is the earliest known work of art to mention <b>Kim Il Sung</b>, and so can be said to mark the beginning of the personality cult built around him.</blockquote><a href="/songs/song-of-general-kim-il-sung">Song of General Kim Il Sung</a></li><li><blockquote>It praises Kim Jong Il, who ruled from 1994 to 2011 and is styled the &quot;Eternal General Secretary&quot;, as part of his personality cult; under his father <b>Kim Il Sung</b> he had himself helped to run the cult of the elder Kim.</blockquote><a href="/songs/song-of-general-kim-jong-il">Song of General Kim Jong Il</a></li><li><blockquote>She sings the song when she finds that the hospital to which she is escorting patients has moved, and in it she voices her lifelong wish to meet her leader, the &quot;dear general&quot; <b>Kim Il Sung</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/where-are-you-dear-general">Where Are You, Dear General?</a></li><li><blockquote>In February 1946 the Soviet Civil Administration convened a meeting of political parties and local people's committees in Pyongyang, which created the Provisional People's Committee for North Korea, with <b>Kim Il Sung</b> as chairman and Kim Tu-bong as vice chairman.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-peoples-committee-for-north-korea">Provisional People's Committee for North Korea</a></li></ul></section>
